---
import AppLayout from '../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import medicines from '../data/medicines.json';
import meta from '../data/meta.json';
import { SYMPTOMS } from '../data/taxonomy.mjs';

const tagged = medicines.filter((m) => m.symptoms.length).length;
---

<AppLayout
	title="About the data — OTC Medicine Advisor"
	description="Where this catalog's information comes from, how symptom matching works, and what its limitations are."
	current="about"
>
	<div class="wrap detail">
		<p class="last-updated" id="fda-stamp" data-generated={meta.generatedAt}>
			<span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Last pulled from FDA — {meta.displayStamp}
		</p>

		<script is:inline>
			// A deployed copy can sit untouched for months. The launch-time check
			// can't help there, so say so plainly rather than showing a stale date
			// as though it were current.
			(() => {
				const stamp = document.getElementById('fda-stamp');
				const generated = new Date(stamp?.dataset.generated ?? '');
				if (!stamp || Number.isNaN(generated.getTime())) return;
				const days = Math.floor((Date.now() - generated.getTime()) / 86400000);
				if (days < 30) return;
				stamp.classList.add('last-updated--stale');
				const note = document.createElement('span');
				note.className = 'stale-note';
				note.textContent = `${days} days ago — refresh due`;
				stamp.append(note);
			})();
		</script>

		<h1>About the data</h1>

		<section class="section">
			<h2><span aria-hidden="true">🏛️</span>Everything here comes from the FDA</h2>
			<p>
				Every medicine page is built from the U.S. Food &amp; Drug Administration's
				<a href="https://open.fda.gov/apis/drug/label/" rel="noopener">openFDA drug label API</a> —
				the same "Drug Facts" text that is legally required on the package. The wording in the
				<em>Uses</em>, <em>Directions</em> and <em>Warnings</em> sections is the manufacturer's own,
				reproduced rather than rewritten.
			</p>
			<p style="margin-bottom:0">
				We have not written any medical claims ourselves, and we do not rank, rate or recommend one
				product over another.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2><span aria-hidden="true">🔎</span>How symptom search works</h2>
			<p>
				Drug labels are legal documents. They say "temporarily relieves nasal congestion", not
				"unblocks your nose". So there are two layers:
			</p>
			<ul class="label-list">
				<li>
					A <strong>symptom taxonomy</strong> of {SYMPTOMS.length} common complaints, each with the
					everyday phrases people actually type ("stuffy nose", "blocked up", "can't breathe through
					my nose") and the corresponding label terminology.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong>build-time tagging pass</strong> that reads each label's <em>Purpose</em> and
					<em>Uses</em> text and tags the medicine with the symptoms it is officially labelled to
					treat. {tagged} of {medicines.length} medicines carry at least one symptom tag.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p style="margin-bottom:0">
				Your search never leaves your browser — the index is a static file and matching runs
				client-side. There is no analytics, no account, and no server logging what you searched for.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2><span aria-hidden="true">📦</span>What's in the catalog — and what isn't</h2>
			<p>
				openFDA holds well over a hundred thousand OTC labels, but they are overwhelmingly
				duplicates: every supermarket's own-brand ibuprofen files separately. So the catalog is built
				around the {medicines.length} most widely-marketed <em>active ingredients</em>, with one
				well-documented label representing each, and brand names collected across all of them.
			</p>
			<p style="margin-bottom:0">
				Deliberately excluded: sunscreens, antiperspirants, toothpaste and hand sanitiser. The FDA
				classes these as OTC drugs, but nobody searching "my head hurts" needs them.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="section section--warn">
			<h2><span aria-hidden="true">⚠️</span>Known limitations</h2>
			<ul class="label-list">
				<li>
					<strong>One label per ingredient.</strong> Strengths, formulations and age limits vary
					between products. The page is a guide; the box in your hand is authoritative.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>United States only.</strong> Availability and brand names differ elsewhere, and
					some medicines sold OTC here need a prescription in other countries.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No interaction checking.</strong> This site does not know what else you take and
					cannot warn you about combinations. A pharmacist can.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Symptom matching is approximate.</strong> It matches wording, not diagnosis. It
					cannot tell a tension headache from a migraine, or heartburn from something cardiac.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Labels change.</strong> Data is fetched at build time, so it is a snapshot. Each
					medicine page shows its label version date and links to the live official record on
					DailyMed.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2><span aria-hidden="true">⚖️</span>Independence</h2>
			<p style="margin-bottom:0">
				This site is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to the FDA, any pharmacy, or any
				manufacturer. Retailer links are plain searches with no affiliate tracking, and no product is
				promoted in exchange for payment.
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
</AppLayout>
